{{Politics of East Timor}}

Presidential elections were held in [[East Timor]] for the first time on 14 April 2002 under [[United Nations Transitional Administration in East Timor|UN administration]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/asia-pacific/1765674.stm|title=UN sets East Timor election date|publisher=BBC News|date=17 January 2002|access-date=1 July 2007}}</ref> As expected, the former president of the East Timorese resistance movement, Conselho Nacional da Resistencia Timorense, [[Xanana Gusmão]], running as an independent candidate but with the unofficial support of small opposition parties like the Democratic Party, won a large majority.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsvote.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/talking_point/1930828.stm|title=East Timor has elected its first president, paving the way for it to become the world's newest country on 20 May|publisher=BBC News|date=19 April 2002|access-date=1 July 2007|archive-date=5 July 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190705104641/http://newsvote.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/talking_point/1930828.stm|url-status=dead}}</ref> The election free and fair.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Smith |first=Anthony L |date=2004 |title=East Timor: Elections in the World's Newest Nation |url=https://doi.org/10.1353/jod.2004.0036 |journal=Journal of Democracy |volume=15 |issue=2 |pages=145–159 |doi=10.1353/jod.2004.0036 |issn=1086-3214|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Gusmão only had one challenger, former [[FRETILIN]] president [[Francisco Xavier do Amaral]], who had been sworn in as president when East Timor declared independence on 28 November 1975, though his term was short-lived as Indonesia invaded on 5 December. Do Amaral, now head of a new party, the ASDT, had admitted that he fully expected to lose to Gusmão, but felt that running was symbolically important, demonstrating the strength of East Timor's democracy by ensuring that Gusmão had competition in the race.

